2️⃣ OpenShift OOMKilled (Kernel OOM Killer)
When it happens
==================
Total process memory > Pod memory limit
(heap + native)
==================

⚠️ Heap may NOT be full
==================
Native memory grows
↓
Total memory crosses cgroup limit
↓
Kernel SIGKILLs process
↓
No GC, no exception
==============
How it manifests
============
Process killed instantly
↓
Pod restarts
↓
Traffic blips
===============
What you see in application logs
==============
❌ Nothing useful
No stacktrace
No OOME
Logs just stop
===========
What you see in pod status
    oc describe pod <pod>

    Last State:
      Terminated
      Reason: OOMKilled

Restart count increments.

What you see in events
    oc get events
    Killing container lending-app due to OOM

What you see in Grafana / Prometheus
=============================
Container memory
    container_memory_working_set_bytes ≈ memory limit


ASCII:

Container Memory
|
|        ██████████████████████  ← hits limit
|        ██████████████████████
|        ██████████████████████
+----------------------------------> time

JVM heap
Heap used may be only 60–70%


➡️ This is the confusing part:

“Heap looks fine, but pod died.”

Typical causes

MaxRAMPercentage too high (70–80%)

Netty / WebClient direct buffers

Too many threads (stack memory)

TLS + HTTP/2 buffers

Metaspace growth

JNI / native libs

Side-by-side comparison (bookmark this)
┌──────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┐
│ Java OOME            │ OpenShift OOMKilled      │
├──────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┤
│ Trigger              │ Heap full                │ Memory limit exceeded
│ Who kills?           │ JVM                      │ Linux kernel
│ GC involved?         │ Yes                      │ No
│ Exception in logs?   │ Yes (OOME)               │ No
│ Pod restarts?        │ No (usually)             │ Yes
│ Heap at max?         │ Yes                      │ Maybe not
│ Fix                  │ Tune heap / code         │ Lower heap %, add headroom
└──────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┘

How to quickly identify which one happened
Step 1 — Pod state
oc describe pod <pod>


If you see:

Reason: OOMKilled


➡️ Kernel OOM

Step 2 — App logs

If you see:

java.lang.OutOfMemoryError


➡️ Java OOME

Step 3 — Grafana dashboard clues
Symptom	Likely cause
Heap flat at Xmx	Java OOME
Container memory hits limit	OOMKilled
GC pauses spike	Java OOME
Sudden pod restart	OOMKilled
Production-grade prevention rules (lending apps)
Memory sizing

Heap ≤ 55–60% of pod memory limit

Never 75% unless you really know native usage

Monitoring to enable

container_memory_working_set_bytes

jvm_memory_used_bytes

jvm_gc_pause_seconds

pod restart count

Interview-ready summary (use verbatim)

“Java OOME happens when the heap is exhausted and GC can’t reclaim memory; it shows up as an exception and the pod keeps running. OOMKilled happens when total process memory exceeds the pod limit; the kernel kills the process instantly with no Java exception, and the pod restarts.”

